const life="Edward Milo Webb was born on 17 August 1815 in Hanover, Chautauqua County, New York, the son of James Webb and Hannah Griswold. Western New York was still being settled by families moving out from older communities to the east. His own household later followed a far longer migration across the developing United States, from the Great Lakes and Mississippi Valley toward the western plains.\n\nEdward married Caroline Amelia Owens on 21 December 1839, when he was twenty-four and she was eighteen. They began their family amid the instability and frequent moves of the Latter-day Saint settlements in Missouri and Illinois. Their lives were not confined to the places where their children were born. Edward repeatedly traveled to work with scattered communities, while Caroline kept their household together through changes of residence.\n\nEdward's own published words give the clearest view of those years. A letter in the Times and Seasons in 1843 describes his departure from Nauvoo around 3 October 1842 with H. B. Owens and a journey through Niles and Albion, Michigan, to Comstock, near Kalamazoo. He wrote about meeting families and organizing a local congregation. This account places him on the roads of western Michigan well before the famous overland journey of 1852, and shows his capacity to travel, communicate and organize.\n\nOther contemporary documents show that he remained involved in Michigan the following year. Minutes of a gathering at Comstock on 1–2 June 1844 name E. M. Webb as both a clerk and a speaker. He thus appears not merely as a traveler remembered by descendants but as a participant whose words and administrative work were recorded at the time. BYU scholars have also quoted verse from an Edward Milo Webb journal preserved in Special Collections. The journal's original pages have not yet been examined, but the citation points to a man who wrote and kept a record of his experiences.\n\nBy 1850 Edward and Caroline were living in Clinton County, Missouri, where an enumerated household includes E. M. Webb, C. A. Webb and their children. In 1852 the family joined the wagon company led by Uriah Curtis. A newspaper list printed in September names Edward M. Webb, his wife and five children in that company. A more detailed traveling-group record identifies four young children traveling with the couple—Cordelia, Marcellus, Edward Jr. and Caroline—along with Edward's younger sister Mary Jane Webb. The newspaper's short description should not be mistaken for proof that five of the people traveling were Edward and Caroline's biological children.\n\nThe westward journey became a family catastrophe. Lucy Benson Wilson, another member of the Curtis company, later recalled several deaths from cholera during July 1852. Family accounts report that Edward himself contracted the disease and died near the Platte River on 31 July, aged thirty-six. His son Edward Jr., only five at the time, later preserved memories of wagons, livestock and the crossing. A separate diary from another emigrant company records passing Curtis's company on 1 August, but does not witness Edward's death. The place of his grave has not been established, and a precise modern county or burial location cannot responsibly be assigned.\n\nCaroline continued to Utah with the children and gave birth to their son Francis Adelbert Webb in March 1853, after Edward's death. Edward's life was brief, but the records preserve more of him than a simple pioneer death entry: a husband and father, an articulate correspondent, a clerk and speaker in Michigan, and a man whose ambitions carried him across a changing country before the journey ended beside the Platte.";
const historicalContext="Hanover stood in western New York's Chautauqua County, in a region transformed during Edward's childhood by migration, clearing of forest land and expanding transportation. The Erie Canal opened in 1825 and helped redirect traffic, goods and people across upstate New York. His generation entered adulthood during rapid growth in Ohio, Michigan and Missouri, as farming settlements spread and new roads connected inland communities.\n\nIn the 1830s and 1840s the Latter-day Saint movement's settlements faced serious conflicts in Missouri and later Illinois. Families continued to move while organizing new congregations and seeking security. In western Michigan, places such as Niles, Albion and Comstock were small growing towns amid farms and new transport routes. Edward's personal 1843 letter and 1844 published meeting minutes place him in those particular communities.\n\nThe 1852 overland route carried emigrants along the Platte River toward Utah, with repeated river crossings, scarce medical help and large groups of people and livestock sharing camps. Cholera was a constant danger, and members of Uriah Curtis's company left firsthand recollections of deaths on the journey. A separate period diary puts the Curtis company on the North Platte route in early August but does not identify the site of Edward's death or burial.";
